{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.6.6","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/rizviabiha/chains?scriptVersionId=366190090\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n\n# Chains in LangChain\n\n## 1. Definition\n\nA Chain in LangChain is a sequence of interconnected components that work together to perform a specific task. The output of one component is passed as input to the next component.\n\nFor example, a basic chain connects a Prompt Template with an LLM to generate a response.\n\n```\nUser Input\n    |\n    v\nPrompt Template\n    |\n    v\nLLM\n    |\n    v\nOutput\n```\n\n## 2. Why do we need Chains?\n\nIn an LLM application, we often need to perform multiple operations to complete a task.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Accept user input.\n- Format the input using a prompt template.\n- Send the prompt to an LLM.\n- Process the generated response.\n- Return the final output.\n\nChains help us:\n\n1. Connect multiple components.\n2. Organize complex workflows.\n3. Pass outputs between different components.\n4. Reuse workflows.\n5. Simplify the development of LLM applications.\n\n## 3. Classification / Types of Chains\n\nFor learning purposes, we can classify the commonly discussed chains into three broad categories.\n\n## Types of Chains in LangChain\n\n1\\. Basic Chain\n\n\\- LLM Chain\n\n2\\. Composition / Control-flow Chains\n\n- Simple Sequential Chain\n- Sequential Chain\n- Router Chain\n\n3\\. Document Processing Chains\n\n- Stuff Documents Chain\n- Map Reduce Documents Chain\n- Refine Documents Chain\n\nThis is a conceptual classification for organizing your notes, not an official LangChain taxonomy.\n\n### A. LLM Chain\n\nThe most basic chain. It connects a Prompt Template with an LLM.\n\nWorking:\n\n```\nInput → Prompt Template → LLM → Output\n```\n\nExample: Generate an explanation of a given topic.\n\n```\nfrom langchain.chains import LLMChainchain = LLMChain(    llm=llm,    prompt=prompt)response = chain.run(\"Machine Learning\")\n```\n\n`LLMChain` is a legacy LangChain class. It is useful to understand when studying older tutorials.\n\n### B. Sequential Chain\n\nA Sequential Chain executes multiple chains one after another.\n\nThe output of the first chain becomes the input of the next chain.\n\nExample: Generate a title and then generate a story using that title.\n\n```\nUser Topic\n    |\n    v\nChain 1: Generate Title\n    |\n    v\nGenerated Title\n    |\n    v\nChain 2: Generate Story\n    |\n    v\nFinal Story\n```\n\nIt has two commonly discussed variations:\n\n1\\. SimpleSequentialChain\n\n- Passes the output of one chain to the next.\n- Generally works with one input and one output.\n\n2\\. SequentialChain\n\n- Supports multiple input and output variables.\n- Suitable for more complex workflows.\n\n### C. Router Chain\n\nA Router Chain decides which chain should execute based on the user's input.\n\nExample: An educational chatbot that handles mathematics, science, and programming questions.\n\n```\n             User Input\n                 |\n                 v\n               Router\n                 |\n        +--------+--------+\n        |        |        |\n        v        v        v\n      Math    Science  Programming\n      Chain    Chain      Chain\n        |        |         |\n        +--------+---------+\n                 |\n                 v\n             Final Output\n```\n\nMain purpose: Dynamically route input to the appropriate chain.\n\n### D. Stuff Documents Chain\n\nUsed when we have multiple documents and want to provide all of them to an LLM.\n\nIt combines all documents into one prompt and sends that prompt to the LLM.\n\nWorking:\n\n```\nDocument 1 ─┐\nDocument 2 ─┼──> Combine Documents\nDocument 3 ─┘          |\n                       v\n                      LLM\n                       |\n                       v\n                 Final Answer\n```\n\nAdvantage: Simple and requires fewer LLM calls.\n\nDisadvantage: All documents must fit within the LLM's context window.\n\n### E. Map Reduce Documents Chain\n\nUsed when dealing with a large collection of documents.\n\nIt has two main stages:\n\n- Map: Processes each document independently and generates an output for each.\n- Reduce: Combines the individual outputs into a final result.\n\nWorking:\n\n```\n        Multiple Documents\n                |\n                v\n            MAP STAGE\n                |\n       Process Each Document\n                |\n                v\n        Individual Outputs\n                |\n                v\n           REDUCE STAGE\n                |\n                v\n          Final Output\n```\n\nAdvantage: Can process large collections of documents.\n\nDisadvantage: Requires multiple LLM calls, increasing cost and latency.\n\n### F. Refine Documents Chain\n\nUsed to generate an answer progressively by processing documents one after another.\n\nIt first generates an initial answer using the first document. Then it improves that answer using the next document.\n\nWorking:\n\n```\nDocument 1\n    |\n    v\nInitial Answer\n    |\n    v\nDocument 2 + Initial Answer\n    |\n    v\nRefined Answer\n    |\n    v\nDocument 3 + Refined Answer\n    |\n    v\nFinal Answer\n```\n\nAdvantage: The answer gets progressively updated using additional information.\n\nDisadvantage: Sequential processing makes it slower.\n\n## 4. Difference between Document Processing Chains\n\nThis comparison is particularly important.\n\n| Feature         | Stuff                     | Map Reduce              | Refine                 |\n| --------------- | ------------------------- | ----------------------- | ---------------------- |\n| Processing      | All documents together    | Documents independently | One after another      |\n| LLM calls       | Usually one               | Multiple                | Multiple               |\n| Large documents | Limited by context window | Suitable                | Suitable               |\n| Processing      | Single combined prompt    | Map then reduce         | Progressive refinement |\n| Speed           | Generally faster          | Can be parallelized     | Generally slower       |\n\n## 5. Important points to remember\n\n1. Chains connect multiple components to perform a task.\n2. LLM Chain is the basic form of chain.\n3. Sequential Chain executes multiple chains in a predefined order.\n4. Router Chain selects an appropriate chain based on input.\n5. Stuff, Map Reduce, and Refine are document-combination strategies.\n6. Document processing chains are particularly useful in document-based question answering and RAG applications.\n7. Older LangChain tutorials frequently use classes such as `LLMChain`, `SequentialChain`, and `StuffDocumentsChain`. These are legacy APIs, but their underlying concepts remain useful.\n\n## 6. Interview questions\n\n- What is a chain in LangChain?\n- Why are chains required?\n- What is LLMChain?\n- What is the difference between SimpleSequentialChain and SequentialChain?\n- What is RouterChain?\n- Explain Stuff, Map Reduce, and Refine.\n- What is the difference between Map Reduce and Refine?\n- Which document chain would you choose for processing a large collection of documents and why?\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Example of Simple Sequential Chain","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv\nfrom langchain_core.prompts import PromptTemplate\nfrom langchain_core.output_parsers import StrOutputParser\n\nload_dotenv()\n\nprompt = PromptTemplate(\n    template='Generate 5 interesting facts about {topic}',\n    input_variables=['topic']\n)\n\nmodel = ChatOpenAI()\n\nparser = StrOutputParser()\n\nchain = prompt | model | parser\n\nresult = chain.invoke({'topic':'cricket'})\n\nprint(result)\n\nchain.get_graph().print_ascii()","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Example  of Sequential Chain\n\n# Sequential Chain\n\n## 1. Definition\n\nA Sequential Chain in LangChain is a chain in which multiple processing steps are executed one after another in a predefined order. The output generated by one step is passed as input to the next step.\n\nIt is useful when a task requires multiple operations to be performed sequentially to produce the final result.\n\n## 2. Working\n\nConsider an application that generates a detailed report and then creates a summary of that report.\n\n```\nUser Input (Topic)\n        |\n        v\nPrompt Template 1\n(Generate Detailed Report)\n        |\n        v\n      LLM\n        |\n        v\n  Output Parser\n        |\n        v\nGenerated Detailed Report\n        |\n        v\nPrompt Template 2\n(Generate 5-point Summary)\n        |\n        v\n      LLM\n        |\n        v\n  Output Parser\n        |\n        v\n    Final Summary\n```\n\n\n\nThe pipe (`|`) operator connects all components in sequence.\n\nWhat happens:\n\n1. `prompt1` receives the topic.\n2. The first model generates a detailed report.\n3. `parser` extracts the report as a string.\n4. `prompt2` receives that report as its `{text}` variable.\n5. The second model generates a five-point summary.\n6. The final parser extracts the summary as a string.\n\n\n## 5. Why is it called a Sequential Chain?\n\nBecause multiple operations are performed one after another, and each step depends on the output of the previous step.\n\nIn this example:\n\n- First operation: Generate a detailed report.\n- Second operation: Generate a summary using that report.\n\nThe second operation cannot perform its intended task until the first operation produces the report.\n\n## 6. Simple Chain vs Sequential Chain\n\n| Simple Chain                            | Sequential Chain           |\n| --------------------------------------- | -------------------------- |\n| Basic workflow                          | Multiple processing steps  |\n| Usually performs a straightforward task | Performs a multi-step task |\n| Example: Prompt → LLM → Parser          | Example: Report → Summary  |\n| Fewer processing stages                 | Multiple connected stages  |\n\nImportant note: Your code uses modern LangChain composition syntax. Older LangChain tutorials also introduce `SimpleSequentialChain` and `SequentialChain` classes. Your example demonstrates the sequential workflow concept; it does not instantiate either of those legacy classes.\n\nRemember for interviews\n\nA Sequential Chain connects multiple processing steps in a predefined order, where the output of one step becomes the input of the next step. It is useful for applications that require multiple dependent LLM operations, such as generating a detailed report and then summarizing it.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv\nfrom langchain_core.prompts import PromptTemplate\nfrom langchain_core.output_parsers import StrOutputParser\n\nload_dotenv()\n\nprompt1 = PromptTemplate(\n    template='Generate a detailed report on {topic}',\n    input_variables=['topic']\n)\n\nprompt2 = PromptTemplate(\n    template='Generate a 5 pointer summary from the following text \\n {text}',\n    input_variables=['text']\n)\n\nmodel = ChatOpenAI()\n\nparser = StrOutputParser()\n\nchain = prompt1 | model | parser | prompt2 | model | parser\n\nresult = chain.invoke({'topic': 'Unemployment in India'})\n\nprint(result)\n\nchain.get_graph().print_ascii()","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n\nA Router Chain is a type of conditional workflow. However, the terms are not necessarily interchangeable.\n\n### 1. Router Chain\n\nA Router Chain is a chain that analyzes the input and decides which destination chain should process it.\n\nExample: An educational chatbot that handles different subjects.\n\n```\n          User Question\n                |\n                v\n              Router\n                |\n       +--------+--------+\n       |        |        |\n       v        v        v\n     Math    Science   Coding\n     Chain    Chain    Chain\n```\n\nThe router selects the appropriate chain based on the user's question.\n\n### 2. Conditional Chain\n\nA Conditional Chain is a general workflow in which the next operation is selected based on a condition.\n\nExample:\n\n```\n       User Input\n            |\n            v\n       Check Condition\n            |\n       +----+----+\n       |         |\n    If True   If False\n       |         |\n       v         v\n    Chain A   Chain B\n```\n\nThe condition could be based on:\n\n- User input.\n- A particular value.\n- A classification result.\n- A logical expression.\n\n### 3. Difference\n\n| Router Chain                             | Conditional Chain                            |\n| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| Selects an appropriate destination chain | Executes different paths based on conditions |\n| Often uses input classification          | Can use simple logical conditions            |\n| A specific routing pattern               | A broader conditional execution concept      |\n\n### What should you write in your notes?\n\n> Router Chain: A chain that analyzes user input and dynamically routes it to the most appropriate destination chain.\n>\n> Conditional Chain: A workflow that selects its execution path based on a specified condition.\n\nRemember: Every Router Chain involves conditional decision-making, but not every conditional workflow is a Router Chain.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n# Conditional Chain\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI\nfrom langchain_anthropic import ChatAnthropic\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv\nfrom langchain_core.prompts import PromptTemplate\nfrom langchain_core.output_parsers import StrOutputParser\nfrom langchain.schema.runnable import RunnableParallel, RunnableBranch, RunnableLambda\nfrom langchain_core.output_parsers import PydanticOutputParser\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\nfrom typing import Literal\n\nload_dotenv()\n\nmodel = ChatOpenAI()\n\nparser = StrOutputParser()\n\nclass Feedback(BaseModel):\n\n    sentiment: Literal['positive', 'negative'] = Field(description='Give the sentiment of the feedback')\n\nparser2 = PydanticOutputParser(pydantic_object=Feedback)\n\nprompt1 = PromptTemplate(\n    template='Classify the sentiment of the following feedback text into postive or negative \\n {feedback} \\n {format_instruction}',\n    input_variables=['feedback'],\n    partial_variables={'format_instruction':parser2.get_format_instructions()}\n)\n\nclassifier_chain = prompt1 | model | parser2\n\nprompt2 = PromptTemplate(\n    template='Write an appropriate response to this positive feedback \\n {feedback}',\n    input_variables=['feedback']\n)\n\nprompt3 = PromptTemplate(\n    template='Write an appropriate response to this negative feedback \\n {feedback}',\n    input_variables=['feedback']\n)\n\nbranch_chain = RunnableBranch(\n    (lambda x:x.sentiment == 'positive', prompt2 | model | parser),\n    (lambda x:x.sentiment == 'negative', prompt3 | model | parser),\n    RunnableLambda(lambda x: \"could not find sentiment\")\n)\n\nchain = classifier_chain | branch_chain\n\nprint(chain.invoke({'feedback': 'This is a beautiful phone'}))\n\nchain.get_graph().print_ascii()","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Parallel Chain Example","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI\nfrom langchain_anthropic import ChatAnthropic\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv\nfrom langchain_core.prompts import PromptTemplate\nfrom langchain_core.output_parsers import StrOutputParser\nfrom langchain.schema.runnable import RunnableParallel\n\nload_dotenv()\n\nmodel1 = ChatOpenAI()\n\nmodel2 = ChatAnthropic(model_name='claude-3-7-sonnet-20250219')\n\nprompt1 = PromptTemplate(\n    template='Generate short and simple notes from the following text \\n {text}',\n    input_variables=['text']\n)\n\nprompt2 = PromptTemplate(\n    template='Generate 5 short question answers from the following text \\n {text}',\n    input_variables=['text']\n)\n\nprompt3 = PromptTemplate(\n    template='Merge the provided notes and quiz into a single document \\n notes -> {notes} and quiz -> {quiz}',\n    input_variables=['notes', 'quiz']\n)\n\nparser = StrOutputParser()\n\nparallel_chain = RunnableParallel({\n    'notes': prompt1 | model1 | parser,\n    'quiz': prompt2 | model2 | parser\n})\n\nmerge_chain = prompt3 | model1 | parser\n\nchain = parallel_chain | merge_chain\n\ntext = \"\"\"\nSupport vector machines (SVMs) are a set of supervised learning methods used for classification, regression and outliers detection.\n\nThe advantages of support vector machines are:\n\nEffective in high dimensional spaces.\n\nStill effective in cases where number of dimensions is greater than the number of samples.\n\nUses a subset of training points in the decision function (called support vectors), so it is also memory efficient.\n\nVersatile: different Kernel functions can be specified for the decision function. Common kernels are provided, but it is also possible to specify custom kernels.\n\nThe disadvantages of support vector machines include:\n\nIf the number of features is much greater than the number of samples, avoid over-fitting in choosing Kernel functions and regularization term is crucial.\n\nSVMs do not directly provide probability estimates, these are calculated using an expensive five-fold cross-validation (see Scores and probabilities, below).\n\nThe support vector machines in scikit-learn support both dense (numpy.ndarray and convertible to that by numpy.asarray) and sparse (any scipy.sparse) sample vectors as input. However, to use an SVM to make predictions for sparse data, it must have been fit on such data. For optimal performance, use C-ordered numpy.ndarray (dense) or scipy.sparse.csr_matrix (sparse) with dtype=float64.\n\"\"\"\n\nresult = chain.invoke({'text':text})\n\nprint(result)\n\nchain.get_graph().print_ascii()\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null}]}